\part{LineScope for Windows}

\chapter{The Windows Port}\label{ch:win-overview}

Everything so far described LineScope as a macOS program. This part describes its second life: a
native Windows version, written in C\# for .NET~9 with the Windows Presentation Foundation (WPF)
and the Windows Imaging Component (WIC)~\cite{wpf}. It has the same three zones, the same shared
line, the same chained operations, split panes, test patterns and inspector. More importantly, it
computes the same numbers.

\section{Why a native port}

LineScope is a tool for looking closely at pixels, and that purpose rules out the easy ways of
running it on Windows. A cross-platform toolkit would put a layer of its own between the image and
the screen, with its own scaling and color handling, exactly where the program must be precise.
Rewriting the app in a web technology would have the same problem, and would lose the fast
numeric loops. The Swift code itself can't be reused: SwiftUI, Core Image, vDSP and Swift Charts
exist only on Apple platforms.

So the Windows version is a port in the old sense: the same design, rewritten in the native
language and framework of the other platform. C\# is close enough to Swift that most files
translate almost line by line. It has value types, generics, closures, [[async]]/[[await]], and
pattern matching. WPF draws with Direct3D and knows about per-monitor DPI, so one image pixel
can be put on exactly one screen pixel.

\section{The solution}

A .NET \emph{solution} groups projects, much as the Xcode project groups a package and an app.
The Windows solution has three, one for each part of the macOS code base:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
macOS & Windows & Contents \\
\midrule
[[Packages/LineScopeCore]] & [[Windows/LineScope.Core]] & numeric core, no user interface \\
[[Packages/LineScopeCore/Tests]] & [[Windows/LineScope.Core.Tests]] & unit tests (xUnit) \\
[[LineScope]] (the app) & [[Windows/LineScope.App]] & documents, session, windows, menus \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The core project targets plain [[net9.0]] and references nothing from WPF, just as
[[LineScopeCore]] imports no SwiftUI. The app project depends on it; the test project depends only
on the core. Inside each project the files mirror the Swift ones: [[PixelBuffer.swift]] becomes
[[PixelBuffer.cs]], [[Resampling.swift]] becomes [[Resampling.cs]], and so on, with the type and
member names kept and only the capitalization adapted to .NET conventions
([[TestPatternGenerator.make]] becomes [[TestPatternGenerator.Make]], the property [[lineage]]
becomes [[Lineage]]).

Where the macOS version leaned on an Apple framework, the port needs a replacement. The table
summarizes them; the chapters that follow explain each one where it occurs.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.36\textwidth}p{0.58\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
macOS & Windows \\
\midrule
SwiftUI [[DocumentGroup(viewing:)]] & one [[MainWindow]] per document, opened by the Open dialog,
  drag and drop or the command line; the app quits with its last document window \\
Core Graphics and ImageIO decoding & WIC through [[BitmapDecoder]], converted to premultiplied
  8-bit BGRA, embedded ICC profiles converted to sRGB \\
Core Image filters & CPU implementations in [[Filters.cs]] \\
vDSP real FFT & an iterative radix-2 complex FFT, with the same normalization \\
Swift Charts & [[ChartView]], drawn with WPF's [[DrawingContext]] \\
[[@Observable]] [[Session]] & a plain class with a [[Changed]] event carrying flags \\
[[@FocusedValue]] menus & a menu bar per window, bound to that window's session \\
points and Retina pixels & 1 image pixel = 1 device-independent pixel (1/96 inch), and a
  per-monitor DPI-aware process \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Same algorithms, different plumbing}

One rule governs the whole port: \emph{the algorithms stay identical; only the plumbing around them
changes}. The resampling kernels and their widening when downscaling, the footprint weights of the
area method, the bilinear line sampler with its $\lceil L\rceil+1$ samples, the color conversions,
the window functions and the single-sided amplitude normalization, the test-pattern formulas and
the SplitMix64 generator are all transcribed, not reinvented. The same inputs therefore give the
same outputs, to floating-point precision, on both systems, and a spectrum measured on a Mac can be
compared with one measured on a PC.

Two pieces could not be transcribed, because on macOS they were not LineScope's code at all. The
FFT came from vDSP, and the port brings its own; since only the transform changes and not what is
done with its output, the spectra still agree. The Gaussian, box, median, unsharp-mask and noise
reduction filters came from Core Image, whose exact kernels are not documented; the port
reimplements them on the CPU following Core Image's documented behavior, so their results are
close to, but not bit-identical with, the macOS ones. Chapter~\ref{ch:win-core} gives the details.

Everything above the core is plumbing, and there the port follows the conventions of Windows
rather than imitating the Mac: a menu bar in every window, keyboard shortcuts with Ctrl, the
standard file dialogs, and an application that ends when its last document window closes.

\section{How to read this part}

This part assumes the first. It concentrates on what the port \emph{changes} and why, and refers
back to the macOS chapters for the theory. Where a C\# file is a faithful transcription of a Swift
file, its chunks appear with only a short note and a reference to the chapter that explains them;
where it differs, the difference is discussed. The chunk names all begin with ``Win'', so the index
keeps the two versions apart.

The chapters follow the order of the first part.
\begin{description}[style=nextline]
\item[Chapter~\ref{ch:win-core}] The numeric core, [[LineScope.Core]]: the pixel buffer,
  resampling, filters, sampling, color models, the spectrum with its FFT, and the test patterns.
  It is the counterpart of Chapters~\ref{ch:pixels} to~\ref{ch:patterns}.
\item[Chapter~\ref{ch:win-tests}] The xUnit tests, the counterpart of Chapter~\ref{ch:tests}, with
  new tests for the FFT and the CPU filters.
\item[Chapter~\ref{ch:win-model}] Documents, variants and the session, the counterpart of
  Chapter~\ref{ch:model}.
\item[Chapter~\ref{ch:win-views}] The windows, zones, panes, image canvas, charts, dialogs and the
  inspector, and also the menus, which live in each window. It is the counterpart of
  Chapters~\ref{ch:views} and~\ref{ch:commands}.
\end{description}
The rest of this chapter covers the build files and the application object, the counterpart of the
scenes in [[LineScopeApp]] (Chapter~\ref{ch:model}).

\section{Building}

With the .NET~9 SDK installed, three commands in the [[Windows]] directory build the program, run
it (optionally on an image given on the command line), and run the tests:
\begin{lstlisting}[language={}]
dotnet build LineScope.sln
dotnet run --project LineScope.App [-- image.png]
dotnet test LineScope.Core.Tests
\end{lstlisting}
Visual Studio 2022 can open the same solution; nothing in the build depends on it.

\subsection{The solution file}

The solution file lists the three projects and maps the solution's configurations onto theirs.
Its format is older than .NET and not meant to be written by hand: it is maintained with
[[dotnet sln add]]. The long identifier [[FAE04EC0-...]] is the project-type code for C\#; the other
three are the projects' own. All six solution configurations (Debug and Release, each for Any CPU,
x64 and x86) build the projects as Any CPU, since the code is pure managed code and runs on any
processor. The file starts with an empty line, as Visual Studio writes it.

