\subsection{The app project}

The app's project file is short, because the SDK supplies the defaults. [[WinExe]] builds a
windowed program without a console. The target [[net9.0-windows]] and [[UseWPF]] bring in WPF.
[[Nullable]] turns on the compiler's null-state analysis, which gives C\# much of the safety of
Swift's optionals, and [[ImplicitUsings]] imports the common namespaces ([[System]],
[[System.Linq]], [[System.Collections.Generic]], [[System.Threading.Tasks]]) in every file. The
assembly is named [[LineScope]], so the program is [[LineScope.exe]], and it references the core
project.

The core's project file, with its one notable setting (optimization even in Debug, the counterpart
of the Swift package's [[-O]]), is in Chapter~\ref{ch:win-core}; the test project is in
Chapter~\ref{ch:win-tests}.

<<Windows/LineScope.App/LineScope.App.csproj>>=
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <RootNamespace>LineScope.App</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>LineScope</AssemblyName>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
    <Version>0.1</Version>
    <Product>LineScope</Product>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\LineScope.Core\LineScope.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
@

\subsection{The application manifest}

The manifest matters more than its size suggests. View \textsf{>} Actual Device Pixels promises one
image pixel per screen pixel. A process that doesn't declare itself DPI-aware is drawn by Windows at
96 dots per inch and then stretched as a bitmap to the monitor's scale factor, blurring exactly the
pixels the user is studying. Declaring \emph{per-monitor} awareness tells Windows that WPF handles
scaling itself, and keeps doing so when a window moves to a monitor with a different scale. The
[[dpiAwareness]] element with [[PerMonitorV2]] is read by Windows~10 (version 1703) and later; the
older [[dpiAware]] element, [[true/PM]], is the fallback for earlier systems. The
[[supportedOS]] identifier declares compatibility with Windows~10 and~11, so the program gets their
behavior rather than a compatibility mode.

<<Windows/LineScope.App/app.manifest>>=
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <assemblyIdentity version="0.1.0.0" name="LineScope.app"/>
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <!-- Per-monitor DPI awareness, so "Actual Device Pixels" maps one image pixel to one screen pixel. -->
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/PM</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}"/>
    </application>
  </compatibility>
</assembly>
@

\subsection{Theme information}

The last build file comes from the WPF project template, unchanged. It tells WPF where to look for
theme-specific resources. LineScope defines no custom controls with themed templates, so the
attribute only states that there are no theme dictionaries.

<<Windows/LineScope.App/AssemblyInfo.cs>>=
using System.Windows;

[assembly:ThemeInfo(
    ResourceDictionaryLocation.None,            //where theme specific resource dictionaries are located
                                                //(used if a resource is not found in the page,
                                                // or application resource dictionaries)
    ResourceDictionaryLocation.SourceAssembly   //where the generic resource dictionary is located
                                                //(used if a resource is not found in the page,
                                                // app, or any theme specific resource dictionaries)
)]
@

\section{The application object}

A WPF program is an [[Application]] object, usually described in two halves: a XAML file for its
declarative part and a C\# file for its code. All LineScope's windows are built in C\#
(Chapter~\ref{ch:win-views}), so [[App.xaml]] is the only XAML in the program and it does little.

It sets two things. The first is the shutdown mode. By default a WPF application ends when its main
window, the first one opened, closes. LineScope has no main window: it has any number of document
windows plus two utility windows, the inspector and the pattern generator. With
[[OnExplicitShutdown]] the application ends only when its code says so, which it does when the last
\emph{document} window closes. An inspector left open doesn't keep the program alive, and closing
the first document while others are open doesn't end it. The second is a pair of text styles shared
by the views: a small gray caption and a semibold section header. The caption uses the system's
gray-text brush as a [[DynamicResource]], so it follows the Windows colors, including high-contrast
themes.

There is no [[StartupUri]]: the first window is created in code.

<<Windows/LineScope.App/App.xaml>>=
<Application x:Class="LineScope.App.App"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             ShutdownMode="OnExplicitShutdown">
    <Application.Resources>
        <Style x:Key="CaptionText" TargetType="TextBlock">
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.GrayTextBrushKey}}" />
        </Style>
        <Style x:Key="SectionHeader" TargetType="TextBlock">
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
            <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,12,0,4" />
        </Style>
    </Application.Resources>
</Application>
@

The code half plays the role of [[LineScopeApp]] and its four scenes (Chapter~\ref{ch:model}).
SwiftUI created and tracked the windows of a scene by itself; here the application object does it.

<<Windows/LineScope.App/App.xaml.cs>>=
using System.IO;
using System.Windows;
using LineScope.App.Model;
using LineScope.App.Views;
using LineScope.Core;
using Microsoft.Win32;

namespace LineScope.App;

/// <summary>
/// Application entry: one <see cref="MainWindow"/> per document (image file or test pattern), plus the shared
/// inspector and test-pattern generator windows. The app quits when the last document window closes.
/// </summary>
public partial class App : Application
{
    <<Win App: shared state>>

    <<Win App: startup>>

    <<Win App: creating a document window>>

    <<Win App: the Open dialog>>

    <<Win App: opening a file>>

    <<Win App: opening a test pattern>>

    <<Win App: the inspector and the pattern generator>>
}
@

The application keeps the list of open document windows and the two single utility windows, which
are [[null]] while closed. [[Current]] hides the base class's property of the same name with one that
has the right type, so views can write [[App.Current.OpenFile(...)]] without a cast. The file filter
lists the formats WIC decodes out of the box, plus HEIF and WebP, which it decodes when the Windows
codec extensions are installed; ``All files'' lets the user try anything else.

<<Win App: shared state>>=
public static new App Current => (App)Application.Current;

private readonly List<MainWindow> _documentWindows = [];
private InspectorWindow? _inspector;
private TestPatternWindow? _generator;

public const string OpenFilter =
    "Images|*.png;*.jpg;*.jpeg;*.jpe;*.jfif;*.tif;*.tiff;*.bmp;*.dib;*.gif;*.ico;*.wdp;*.jxr;*.heic;*.heif;*.webp;*.dds|All files|*.*";
@

At startup, every command-line argument that names an existing file is opened in a window of its
own. This is how LineScope is used from Explorer's ``Open with'' and from a shortcut onto which
images are dropped. Without files the app shows one empty document window. On macOS the document
group shows an Open panel instead; an empty window with the menu bar and a hint is the more familiar
Windows behavior (it offers Open and New Test Pattern buttons), and it gives the user a window to drop files on.

<<Win App: startup>>=
protected override void OnStartup(StartupEventArgs e)
{
    base.OnStartup(e);
    var files = e.Args.Where(File.Exists).ToList();
    if (files.Count == 0)
    {
        NewDocumentWindow().Show();
        return;
    }
    foreach (var path in files) OpenFile(path, reuse: null);
}
@

Every document window, whether it will hold a file or a pattern, is created here, so that the
application can count them. When the count reaches zero the application shuts down, which is the
counterpart of the explicit shutdown mode above.

<<Win App: creating a document window>>=
public MainWindow NewDocumentWindow()
{
    var window = new MainWindow();
    _documentWindows.Add(window);
    window.Closed += (_, _) =>
    {
        _documentWindows.Remove(window);
        if (_documentWindows.Count == 0) Shutdown();
    };
    return window;
}
@

File \textsf{>} Open allows several files at once. The first one goes into the window the command
came from if that window is still empty, so opening a file right after launch doesn't leave an empty
window behind; the others get new windows. Drag and drop onto a window calls [[OpenFile]] the same
way (Chapter~\ref{ch:win-views}).

<<Win App: the Open dialog>>=
/// <summary>Shows the Open dialog and opens every chosen file; the first one goes into <paramref name="reuse"/> if it is empty.</summary>
public void ShowOpenDialog(MainWindow? reuse)
{
    var dialog = new OpenFileDialog { Filter = OpenFilter, Multiselect = true, Title = "Open Image" };
    if (dialog.ShowDialog(reuse) != true) return;
    foreach (var path in dialog.FileNames)
    {
        OpenFile(path, reuse);
        reuse = null;
    }
}
@

Opening a file must not freeze the interface, because decoding a large photograph and converting it
to floats takes a noticeable moment. The window is shown at once with a loading message, and the
decoding runs on a thread-pool thread with [[Task.Run]]. [[ImageDocument.Load]] is written to be safe
off the UI thread (Chapter~\ref{ch:win-model}). The [[await]] resumes on the UI thread, where the
window receives the document and builds its session.

The method is [[async void]], the form C\# reserves for fire-and-forget event handlers: nobody
awaits it, so it catches its own exceptions. A file that can't be decoded produces a message box.
The window that was opened for it is then closed, unless it was the empty window being reused or it
is the only window left, in which case closing it would end the application.

<<Win App: opening a file>>=
public async void OpenFile(string path, MainWindow? reuse)
{
    var window = reuse is { HasDocument: false } ? reuse : NewDocumentWindow();
    window.ShowLoading($"Opening {Path.GetFileName(path)}…");
    if (!window.IsVisible) window.Show();
    window.Activate();
    try
    {
        var document = await Task.Run(() => ImageDocument.Load(path));
        window.LoadDocument(document, Path.GetFileName(path));
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        window.ShowEmpty();
        MessageBox.Show(window, $"“{Path.GetFileName(path)}” couldn't be opened.\n\n{ex.Message}", "LineScope",
            MessageBoxButton.OK, MessageBoxImage.Warning);
        // Keep the empty window if it is the only one, so the app doesn't quit.
        if (reuse != window && _documentWindows.Count > 1) window.Close();
    }
}
@

A test pattern gets the same kind of window as a file, exactly as on macOS, where
[[ImageDocument(pattern:)]] let a pattern be shown by the same [[MainView]]. Generating a large
pattern also takes time, so it runs in the background too. A pattern window isn't restored after a
relaunch as it is on macOS; Windows has no such mechanism for ordinary programs.

<<Win App: opening a test pattern>>=
/// <summary>Generates a test pattern in the background and shows it in a new analysis window.</summary>
public async void OpenPattern(TestPatternSpec spec)
{
    var window = NewDocumentWindow();
    window.Title = spec.Title;
    window.ShowLoading($"Generating {spec.Kind.DisplayName()}…");
    window.Show();
    var document = await Task.Run(() => ImageDocument.FromPattern(spec));
    window.LoadDocument(document, spec.Title);
}
@

The inspector and the pattern generator are single windows, like SwiftUI's [[Window]] scenes. Asking
for one that is open brings it to the front; asking for one that was closed creates it again. The
inspector shows whichever session is active (Chapter~\ref{ch:win-model}), so it needs no owner. The
generator opens a little below and to the right of the window it was requested from, so it doesn't
appear somewhere unrelated on a multi-monitor desktop.

<<Win App: the inspector and the pattern generator>>=
public void ShowInspector()
{
    if (_inspector is null)
    {
        _inspector = new InspectorWindow();
        _inspector.Closed += (_, _) => _inspector = null;
        _inspector.Show();
    }
    _inspector.Activate();
}

public void ShowPatternGenerator(Window? owner)
{
    if (_generator is null)
    {
        _generator = new TestPatternWindow();
        if (owner is not null)
        {
            _generator.WindowStartupLocation = WindowStartupLocation.Manual;
            _generator.Left = owner.Left + 60;
            _generator.Top = owner.Top + 60;
        }
        _generator.Closed += (_, _) => _generator = null;
        _generator.Show();
    }
    _generator.Activate();
